{"topicSections":[{"title":"Initializers","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/init(uintValue:)"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Initializers"},{"title":"Instance Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/uintValue"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Properties"},{"title":"Default Implementations","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Comparable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/CustomStringConvertible-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Equatable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Hashable-Implementations","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/UIntRepresentable-Implementations"],"generated":true,"anchor":"Default-Implementations"}],"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL"]]},"metadata":{"title":"MXLNumberLevel","role":"symbol","symbolKind":"struct","fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"roleHeading":"Structure","navigatorTitle":[{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"modules":[{"name":"IvorMXL"}],"externalID":"s:7IvorMXL14MXLNumberLevelV"},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"MXLNumberLevel","kind":"identifier"}],"platforms":["macOS"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2,"anchor":"overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"When polyphonic parts are involved, the ordering within a MusicXML document can differ from musical score","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order. As an example, say we have a piano part in 4\/4 where within a single measure, all the notes on the top","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff are followed by all the notes on the bottom staff. In this example, each staff has a slur that starts on","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"beat 2 and stops on beat 3, and there is a third slur that goes from beat 1 of one staff to beat 4 of the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"other staff.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In this situation, the two mid-measure slurs can use the same number because they do not overlap in MusicXML","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"document order, even though they do overlap in musical score order. Within the MusicXML document, the top","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff slur will both start and stop before the bottom staff slur starts and stops.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If the cross-staff slur starts in the top staff and stops in the bottom staff, it will need a separate number","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"from the mid-measure slurs because it overlaps those slurs in MusicXML document order. However, if the cross-","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"staff slur starts in the bottom staff and stops in the top staff, all three slurs can use the same number.","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"None of them overlap within the MusicXML document, even though they all overlap each other in the musical","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"score order. Within the MusicXML document, the start and stop of the top-staff slur will be followed by the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"stop and start of the cross-staff slur, followed by the start and stop of the bottom-staff slur.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"As this example demonstrates, a reading program should be prepared to handle cases where the number-levels","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"start and stop in an arbitrary order. Because the start and stop values refer to musical score order, a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"program may find the stopping point of an object earlier in the MusicXML document than it will find its","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"starting point.","type":"text"}]}]}],"relationshipsSections":[{"title":"Conforms To","identifiers":["doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SL","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/Se","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SE","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SQ","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s9EscapableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s27ExpressibleByIntegerLiteralP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SH","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s8SendableP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s16SendableMetatypeP","doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/10XestiTools17UIntRepresentableP"],"kind":"relationships","type":"conformsTo"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel"},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel"]}],"sections":[],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Slurs, tuplets, and many other features can be concurrent and overlap within a single musical part. The"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"number-level entity distinguishes up to 16 concurrent objects of the same type when the objects overlap in"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"MusicXML document order. Values greater than 6 are usually only needed for music with a large number of divisi"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"staves in a single part, or if there are more than 6 cross-staff arpeggios in a single measure. When a number-"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"level value is implied, the value is 1 by default."}],"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"patch":0,"minor":3},"references":{"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel/Comparable-Implementations":{"title":"Comparable Implementations","role":"collectionGroup","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Comparable-Implementations","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel\/comparable-implementations","kind":"article"},"doc://IvorMXL/SL":{"title":"Swift.Comparable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SL","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://IvorMXL/SE":{"title":"Swift.Encodable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/SE","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel/uintValue":{"fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"let"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"uintValue"},{"kind":"text","text":": "},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:Su","text":"UInt"}],"kind":"symbol","url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel\/uintvalue","role":"symbol","title":"uintValue","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/uintValue","abstract":[{"text":"Distinguishes up to 16 concurrent objects of the same type when the objects overlap in MusicXML document","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"order.","type":"text"}]},"doc://IvorMXL/s9EscapableP":{"title":"Swift.Escapable","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s9EscapableP","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://IvorMXL/s23CustomStringConvertibleP":{"title":"Swift.CustomStringConvertible","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","type":"unresolvable"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel/Equatable-Implementations":{"title":"Equatable Implementations","role":"collectionGroup","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/\/IvorMXL\/documentation\/IvorMXL\/MXLNumberLevel\/Equatable-Implementations","abstract":[],"url":"\/documentation\/ivormxl\/mxlnumberlevel\/equatable-implementations","kind":"article"},"doc://IvorMXL/documentation/IvorMXL/MXLNumberLevel/UIntRepresentable-Implementations":{"title":"UIntRepresentable Implementations","role":"collectionGroup","type":"topic","identifier":"doc:\/